INSTALL

Install snifd and libsnif from the top directory first, then the relay
    from this one:

        ./configure && make && sudo make install
        cd ca-proxy && ./configure && make && sudo make install

    The relay's programs are here: snif-relay, which runs snifd with this
    tree's pipes, and snif-tunnel, beside the CA proxy daemons.

The two installs make what is missing of:
    - the snif user and group: a system user with no login, home
      /var/lib/snif. An existing snif user is left as it is. The daemons
      run as snif. Add the web server's user (e.g. www-data) to the snif
      group: the PHP endpoints write the CSRs, the approvals and the
      daemons' pipes, and read etc/.
    - /etc/snif/snif.conf, read by snifd and the daemons.
    - /var/lib/snif/ca-proxy, the CA proxy tree: the code (inc/,
      public_html/, secure_html/), the runtime directories and pipes
      (mkdirs below), etc/ linked to /etc/snif/ca-proxy and log/ linked to
      /var/log/snif.
    - the programs in /usr/bin, and the units in /lib/systemd/system:
      snif-relay, snif-tunnel@, snif-certd, snif-apnsd and snif-syncd from
      here, snif-conn from the top directory. None is enabled;
      enable what the host runs, e.g. systemctl enable --now snif-relay
      snif-certd.
    - /etc/cron.d/snif-arc, which runs snif-arc daily (below), and
      /etc/logrotate.d/snif for the daemons' logs. The per-domain
      log/{domain}.log files are not rotated: snif-certd reads their
      modification time as its one-hour retry throttle.

Installing again replaces the code and the units, and keeps snif.conf,
    etc/, the cron and logrotate entries, the state and the logs. etc/ holds the DNS keys, the CA keys and
    the per-domain settings, so its example files are copied only where
    absent. A code directory that the tree links elsewhere, such as a
    release root, is left alone. With DESTDIR the install is staged, e.g.
    for a package: no user is made, nothing is chowned and systemd is not
    reloaded.

mkdirs [base-dir] makes the runtime directories and pipes that are missing,
    under base-dir, else SNIF_CA_PROXY from /etc/snif/snif.conf. The
    install runs it. Run it again after SNIF_CLUSTER_PEERS changes, for
    each peer's pipes and queue: as root it gives what it makes to snif,
    otherwise run it as snif.

The web server is not configured by the install; see public_html/ and
    secure_html/ below.

public_html/ is the HTTP root for catch-all SNIF domains

secure_html/ is the HTTPS root for the dedicated initialization domain,
    such as https://snif.example.com, a custom port such as 4443 is suggested.
    The initialization URL is to be used by SNIF connector on the devices
    to acquire a hostname and a certificate, snifd -a argument.

Crypt::LE is required by bin/snif-certd for Let's Encrypt ACME,
    use cpan install. It is not needed if every domain issues from a local CA
    (see etc/{SNIF_domain}.pub-ca below).

Net::DNS is required if using wildcard certs from Let's Encrypt, use cpan
    install

The openssl command line tool is required by bin/snif-certd for domains that
    issue from a local CA.

etc/{SNIF_initialization_domain}.domains contains the list of {domain} names
    to issue SNIF hosts as subdomains that are expected to point to CNAME
    snif-pool.{domain}, and to be served by HTTP public.html/

etc/{SNIF_domain}.tsig must contain a DDNS TSIG that permits managing IN TXT
    RRs at the name _acme-challenge.{host} is an alias for, for CA domain
    verification: snif-pool.{SNIF_domain}, through the *.{SNIF_domain}
    wildcard, unless DNS says otherwise (several domains can share one pool
    name, see doc/cluster.md). If the alias cannot be followed, snif-certd uses
    snif-pool.{SNIF_domain} in the zone {SNIF_domain}. It adds one TXT value
    per name being validated and sends each update to every nameserver of the
    zone holding that name, with a $DNSUpdateTimeout second timeout. It goes on
    if at least one accepted, waits until every server that answered serves
    the values (up to $DNSWait seconds; a server that refused may be a
    secondary), and afterwards deletes exactly the values it added, so
    concurrent attempts and a certificate for both a name and its wildcard do
    not remove each other's records. A deletion that fails is retried after
    later attempts (var/snif-certd.pending). test/certd-dns.t checks this
    against stub modules.

etc/{SNIF_domain}.pub-ca and etc/{SNIF_domain}.priv-ca (optional) make
    {SNIF_domain} issue from a local CA instead of Let's Encrypt, for closed
    systems whose connectors and relay trust a private CA root rather than the
    public ones. .priv-ca holds the CA private key and .pub-ca the CA
    certificate, both PEM; .pub-ca is a self-signed root, or an intermediate
    followed by the rest of its chain. snif-certd signs each connector's CSR
    itself - no ACME account, no DNS or HTTP challenge - and appends .pub-ca as
    the chain. Certificates are wildcards for the device zone, as with a .tsig
    domain, and are valid for 90 days. A local CA takes precedence over .tsig,
    and has no server-wide fallback: it only issues for the base domain it is
    named for. Keep .priv-ca readable by the snif user only, and consider a
    nameConstraints extension on the CA certificate that permits only
    {SNIF_domain}. The CA root must be trusted by the connectors and by the
    relay for these certificates to verify.

The following per-domain settings are keyed on {SNIF_domain} (the base domain,
    as in the .domains/.tsig files above). Each falls back to a server-wide file
    of the same name without the domain prefix, then to a built-in default, so a
    single-domain relay can use the server-wide file and leave the rest unset.

etc/{SNIF_domain}.keyalgo (optional) sets the private key algorithm the
    connector generates for certificates under this domain: RSA[:bits],
    EC[:curve] (e.g. EC:P-256), ED25519 or ED448. Returned to the connector as
    X-SNIF-KeyAlgo so it can steer local key generation; the proxy never sees
    the key. Falls back to etc/keyalgo, then to the connector default (RSA).

etc/{SNIF_domain}.authurl (optional) overrides the base authorization URL
    advertised to the connector (X-SNIF-AuthUrl) - the HTTPS initialization
    origin it polls, e.g. https://snif.example.com:4443/ . A trailing slash is
    enforced and the certificate CN is appended to it. Falls back to
    etc/authurl, then to the public snif.snif.xyz initialization origin. Set
    this on a self-hosted relay so devices are pointed back at your own origin
    rather than snif.snif.xyz.

etc/{SNIF_domain}.authpage (optional) overrides the interactive authorization
    page a connector's browser is redirected to when no credential is presented.
    The value is the full URL prefix up to and including the query parameter,
    e.g. https://snif.example.com/auth?authUrl= (the connector's polling URL is
    appended). Falls back to etc/authpage, then to https://snif.host/auth .

etc/turnstile.secret and etc/turnstile.sitekey enable the optional Cloudflare
    Turnstile CAPTCHA authorization for the interactive certificate
    authorization page (the in-browser counterpart of VES vaultItem auth).
    turnstile.secret holds the Turnstile secret key; its presence enables the
    CAPTCHA verification in inc/snif-auth-captcha.php, which checks each token
    against it. turnstile.sitekey holds the matching public site key. When BOTH
    files are present the authorization page defaults to a Turnstile challenge,
    whose token is verified against the paired secret; if either is absent the
    page defaults to VES vaultItem authorization. Cloudflare provides a test
    site/secret pair that always passes (1x00000000000000000000AA and
    1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA) for local testing. To use your own
    Turnstile account, replace both files with a matching site/secret pair.

etc/{email}.ves-limit and etc/ves-limit (optional) limit the certificates a
    VES account approves (inc/snif-auth-ves.php). The approvals form a leaky
    bucket: each adds one, one leaks out per refill time, and an approval is
    refused (429) while more than the limit are in it. The file holds the
    limit and the refill time, e.g. "10 1d" (s, m, h or d; a bare number is
    seconds; 0 never refills). {email} is the account's email as VES returns
    it; without that file etc/ves-limit applies, then 10 and 1d. The
    approval times are in auth/ves/{email}, one line each, or in a relay
    cluster auth/ves/{node}/{email}; like the rest of etc/, the limit files
    are written on every node.

snif-certd daemon needs to be launched on the server start to process
    the certificate issuance for SNIF connectors.

snif-arc needs to be launched periodically, once a day: the install adds
    /etc/cron.d/snif-arc for that. It archives aging certificates: it moves any certificate older than
    75 days out of crt/ into a dated folder under arc/. It does not reissue
    anything - removing the stale certificate is what lets snif-certd issue a
    fresh one the next time the connector requests it. A local-CA domain's
    root bundle, crt/{SNIF_domain}.crt next to etc/{SNIF_domain}.pub-ca, is
    not an issued certificate and stays in crt/.

snif-apnsd is an optional process that sends APNS notifications to wake up
    a sleeping iOS device to serve an incoming connection, as long as the
    device has registered with APNS and has communicated the hex APNS ID to
    SNIF relay over the control connection by sending
    "SNIF MSG hostname.snif-1234-5678.example.com apns=0123ABCD..."

snif-syncd keeps the CA proxy state of a relay cluster in step across its
    nodes (doc/cluster.md). It runs only on a node with peers: set
    SNIF_CLUSTER_NODE (this node's name) and SNIF_CLUSTER_PEERS
    ("name=address ...") in /etc/snif/snif.conf. It opens an ssh session to each
    peer (SNIF_CLUSTER_PEER_CMD overrides the command), whose key's forced
    command on the peer is "snif-syncd --serve <this node>". Writers queue
    files with "snif-syncd enqueue <path>"; "snif-syncd status", "reconcile"
    and "seed <peer>" talk to the running daemon. test/syncd.t runs three
    nodes on one host.


CONTENTS:

bin/         The relay (snif-relay, snif-tunnel) and certificate management scripts
etc/         Configuration files
public_html/ Insecure web root for SNIF domains, http://snif-1234-5678.example.com
secure_html/ Secure web root for SNIF initializer, https://snif.example.com:4443
mkdirs       Create directories and pipes for SNIF daemons and scripts
